= Knowledge representation =

== Overview ==
'''Knowledge representation''' (KR) is the study and practice of encoding knowledge in explicit structures that support interpretation, communication, computation and [[Inference|reasoning]].

It is a foundational area of artificial intelligence.

A representation stands in for some aspect of a domain so that a system can reason about the representation instead of interacting directly with the world for every question.

== Representation as surrogate ==
A knowledge representation acts as a '''surrogate''' for the represented domain.

A map represents a territory.

A circuit model represents an electrical system.

An ontology represents categories and relations in a domain.

The representation is useful because operations on it can answer questions about the represented system.

But:

<pre>
representation ≠ represented thing
</pre>

Errors in the representation can produce incorrect reasoning even if the reasoning algorithm is flawless.

== Ontological commitments ==
Davis, Shrobe and Szolovits emphasize that every representation makes '''ontological commitments'''.

Choosing objects, processes, classes, time points, events or causal links determines what the system is prepared to notice and reason about.

A representation is therefore not neutral.

Its primitives encode a view of the world.

== Theory of reasoning ==
A representation also suggests which inferences are important and legitimate.

Examples:

* logic supports deduction;
* frames support inheritance;
* rules support forward/backward chaining;
* probabilistic models support probabilistic inference;
* description logics support subsumption and consistency reasoning.

Representation and reasoning are designed together.

== Efficient computation ==
A representation can make some inferences easy and others difficult.

This creates a trade-off among:
* expressivity;
* tractability;
* storage;
* query efficiency;
* explainability.

A language powerful enough to express arbitrary first-order theories can make general inference undecidable.

Restricted representations can guarantee more predictable computation.

== Human expression ==
Knowledge representation is also a communication medium.

Good representations should help humans:
* inspect assumptions;
* author knowledge;
* understand conclusions;
* debug errors;
* reuse models.

A representation that is theoretically elegant but impossible to maintain can fail as an engineering system.

== Major KR traditions ==
Knowledge representation has used:

* propositional and first-order logic;
* production rules;
* frames;
* [[Semantic network|semantic networks]];
* scripts;
* semantic triples;
* [[Ontology|ontologies]];
* [[Description logic|description logics]];
* probabilistic graphical models;
* [[Knowledge graph|knowledge graphs]].

These approaches have different semantics and inference models.

== Declarative representation ==
A '''declarative''' representation states what is true or what relations hold, rather than prescribing an execution procedure.

For example:

<pre>
Mars instanceOf Planet
</pre>

is declarative.

A program telling a computer exactly how to search for planets is procedural.

Declarative systems allow multiple reasoning engines to operate on the same facts.

== Closed and open worlds ==
Some representations assume missing facts are false or unavailable within a closed database state.

Others, including typical Semantic Web ontology systems, use an open-world assumption:

<pre>
not known true ≠ false
</pre>

The assumption strongly affects inference.

== Monotonicity ==
Classical logics are often monotonic: adding facts cannot invalidate previous logical consequences.

Human commonsense reasoning is often non-monotonic.

Example:

<pre>
birds normally fly
Tweety is a bird
→ Tweety flies

later:
Tweety is a penguin
</pre>

The new information can defeat the default conclusion.

KR research includes defaults, belief revision and non-monotonic logics for such cases.

== Representation and uncertainty ==
Not all knowledge is categorical.

Uncertainty can be represented using:
* probabilities;
* fuzzy values;
* confidence scores;
* alternative hypotheses;
* provenance and evidence.

These are not equivalent methods.

The appropriate representation depends on what uncertainty means in the domain.

== Knowledge representation and databases ==
Databases store structured information.

KR additionally emphasizes explicit [[Semantics|semantics]] and inference.

The boundary is not sharp: modern databases can enforce rich constraints and graph databases can support semantic reasoning.

But a database schema alone is not necessarily a knowledge representation in the stronger AI sense.

== Knowledge representation and machine learning ==
Machine learning often learns numerical representations from data.

Symbolic KR represents explicit entities, concepts and rules.

Modern systems can combine them.

A knowledge graph can use symbolic facts plus embeddings or learned completion models.

These layers should remain distinguishable because their semantics and uncertainty differ.

